{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואני הנה נתתי", :translit "va'ani hineh natati", :gloss "And I, behold, I have given"} {:he "שטר מתנה", :translit "shetar matanah", :gloss "deed of gift"} {:he "בדעת שלימה ובנפש חפצה", :translit "b'da'at shleimah u'venefesh chafetzah", :gloss "with full awareness and willing spirit"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Reads the doubled emphatic language of the verse — the divine speaker 'I, behold, I have given' — as reflecting the formal, wholehearted character of a legal gift transfer."}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'with full awareness and willing spirit' echoes the rabbinic formulation for a valid gift transfer, requiring the giver's complete and uncoerced consent (see Gittin 40a regarding da'at and intent in transfer; Shulchan Arukh, Choshen Mishpat 241:1 on modes of acquisition). Ba'al HaTurim reads God's emphatic first-person language at Numbers 18:8 — 'And I, behold, I have given' — as conveying that the priestly gifts are granted with the same wholehearted, deliberate intent that governs a formal deed of gift under halakhah.", :sources ["Gittin 40a" "Shulchan Arukh Choshen Mishpat 241:1" "Numbers 18:8"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Reads the doubled emphatic language of the verse — the divine speaker 'I, behold, I have given' — as reflecting the formal, wholehearted character of a legal gift transfer.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:29:48.031283758Z", :model "glm-5.2"}